            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} pointed (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            It is in this last form, by “consecutively extending maps over cones”, that the Toda bracket was introduced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Toda62"} Toda 62
          %p
            The more abstract formulation shown at the top, via
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+diagram"} homotopy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+diagrams"} pasting diagrams
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HardieKampsKieboom99"} Hardie-Kamps-Kieboom 99, (0.2)-(0.3)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HardieMarcumOda01"} Hardie-Marcum-Oda 01
            %a{:href => "#HardieKampsMarcum02"} Hardie-Kamps-Marcum 02, (2.2)
            (formalized there inside a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-category"} homotopy 2-category
          %p#GroupActionOnChoicesOfTodaBrackets
            From this abstract homotopy-pasting perspective it is manifest that the set of choices of refined Toda brackets
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TheTodaBracketDependingOnTheNullHomotopies"} (1)
            for given maps
